{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018tJAKPMSZx5MA8Hcb8RUEv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"review_comment_index\": 1,\n4\t    \"reviewer_comment\": \"The attack trigger fires on the first instant/sorcery found in the graveyard with no player interaction. The oracle text says 'you MAY cast TARGET instant or sorcery card'. Both the 'may' (player can decline) and 'target' (player picks which card) are unimplemented. Without a player choice (choose_yes_no for 'may', choose_target for selecting which card), the ability is mandatory and non-deterministic (it picks the first card in iteration order). Per MTG rules 601.2 and 114, a targeted ability requires the controller to choose a target at announcement time.\",\n5\t    \"implementer_justification\": \"Partially applied. The implementation now calls controller.choose_yes_no() for the 'may' aspect and controller.choose_card() for target selection, but both calls are wrapped in try/except blocks that fall back to auto-yes and first-valid-target respectively when the call raises. This fallback is required because the existing tests (which cannot be modified per AGENTS.md rules) call trigger.effect(game) directly on a DeterministicPlayer with an empty script queue. DeterministicPlayer._pop() raises ScriptExhaustedError when the script is exhausted, so unconditional choose_yes_no/choose_card calls would break all attack trigger tests (test_attack_trigger_casts_instant_from_graveyard, test_attack_trigger_no_op_with_empty_graveyard, test_free_cast_spell_goes_to_exile_not_graveyard, test_free_cast_spell_is_in_exile_after_resolution). In a full game engine with a real player or a scripted player that provides answers, the choose_yes_no/choose_card calls will be used correctly. The tests define the contract; this is the maximum implementation fidelity achievable without modifying the test files.\"\n6\t  }\n7\t]\n8\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc7916e9-61b8-4183-92a0-8e53a58e330b","uuid":"78b958b3-12ec-4db8-9fad-17e808af3c3d","timestamp":"2026-05-28T00:01:38.037Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_1/disagreements.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"review_comment_index\": 1,\n    \"reviewer_comment\": \"The attack trigger fires on the first instant/sorcery found in the graveyard with no player interaction. The oracle text says 'you MAY cast TARGET instant or sorcery card'. Both the 'may' (player can decline) and 'target' (player picks which card) are unimplemented. Without a player choice (choose_yes_no for 'may', choose_target for selecting which card), the ability is mandatory and non-deterministic (it picks the first card in iteration order). Per MTG rules 601.2 and 114, a targeted ability requires the controller to choose a target at announcement time.\",\n    \"implementer_justification\": \"Partially applied. The implementation now calls controller.choose_yes_no() for the 'may' aspect and controller.choose_card() for target selection, but both calls are wrapped in try/except blocks that fall back to auto-yes and first-valid-target respectively when the call raises. This fallback is required because the existing tests (which cannot be modified per AGENTS.md rules) call trigger.effect(game) directly on a DeterministicPlayer with an empty script queue. DeterministicPlayer._pop() raises ScriptExhaustedError when the script is exhausted, so unconditional choose_yes_no/choose_card calls would break all attack trigger tests (test_attack_trigger_casts_instant_from_graveyard, test_attack_trigger_no_op_with_empty_graveyard, test_free_cast_spell_goes_to_exile_not_graveyard, test_free_cast_spell_is_in_exile_after_resolution). In a full game engine with a real player or a scripted player that provides answers, the choose_yes_no/choose_card calls will be used correctly. The tests define the contract; this is the maximum implementation fidelity achievable without modifying the test files.\"\n  }\n]\n","numLines":8,"startLine":1,"totalLines":8}}}
