{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015GbaNjcdPPktnxtKH3jpfH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n4\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\",\n5\t    \"line\": 194,\n6\t    \"comment\": \"cast_prepared_spell sets is_prepared = False before checking whether a legal target exists. If _find_any_creature returns None (no creatures on any battlefield), the creature is unprepared but the spell was never actually 'cast' (no effect occurred). Per the Prepared mechanic, casting the copy requires a legal target; if no target exists, the spell cannot be cast and the creature should remain prepared. The fix is to find the target first and only unprepare if a target is found and the spell effect can proceed.\"\n7\t  },\n8\t  {\n9\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n10\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\",\n11\t    \"line\": 130,\n12\t    \"comment\": \"on_resolve is called from _resolve_spell while the creature is still on the stack (before move_to_zone places it on the battlefield). This means the prepared-condition check at line 149-153 evaluates creature counts before Emeritus of Truce itself is on the battlefield. In full MTG rules, ETB triggers resolve after the permanent is on the battlefield. This discrepancy means the controller's creature count is understated by 1 (Emeritus is not counted), making the prepared condition easier to satisfy than it should be. The tests are written around this behavior, but it may be surprising in integration.\"\n13\t  },\n14\t  {\n15\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n16\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\",\n17\t    \"line\": 169,\n18\t    \"comment\": \"The ETB trigger registered by register_triggers watches for EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent with event.permanent is source. In move_to_zone (engine/zones.py), the ETB event is fired BEFORE register_triggers is called. This means the trigger registered here can never fire for the creature's own initial entry — the ETB effect only fires because on_resolve is called directly in _resolve_spell. If the creature re-enters the battlefield via a non-casting path (e.g., a bounce effect that uses move_to_zone directly without going through _resolve_spell), the ETB effect will silently not fire because the trigger is registered after the ETB event. Consider whether the re-entry case is in scope for this card.\"\n19\t  },\n20\t  {\n21\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n22\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\",\n23\t    \"line\": 119,\n24\t    \"comment\": \"get_targets uses Zone.BATTLEFIELD as the zone parameter for a player-targeting TargetRequirement. Players do not reside in any zone — they are not zone-resident objects. If the engine's target validation logic ever checks that the target object is in the specified zone, this will incorrectly reject valid player targets or pass invalid zone checks. A neutral/sentinel zone value (or a dedicated player-targeting API) would be more correct.\"\n25\t  },\n26\t  {\n27\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n28\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\",\n29\t    \"line\": 250,\n30\t    \"comment\": \"_exile_creature_give_life guards life gain with 'if ctrl is not None and power > 0', silently skipping the life-gain step for 0-power creatures. While gaining 0 life is a no-op mechanically, the guard also prevents any life-gain triggered event from firing, which could matter if other effects watch for life-gain events. The spec says 'gains life equal to its power' unconditionally. Consider calling the life-gain path regardless and letting the engine handle a 0-life-gain no-op.\"\n31\t  }\n32\t]\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NZAkrV4jWaxq1LKscLm2HB","session_id":"bc7916e9-61b8-4183-92a0-8e53a58e330b","uuid":"6755f238-ca4f-4575-9b9f-11842546e2ea","timestamp":"2026-05-28T00:21:35.500Z","subagent_type":"Implementer","task_description":"Revision round for sos_13"}
