{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XtEGL4qUfRD9Dg6x4za6g6","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (20KB). Full output saved to: /home/node/.claude/projects/-workspace/bc7916e9-61b8-4183-92a0-8e53a58e330b/tool-results/toolu_01XtEGL4qUfRD9Dg6x4za6g6.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1413-\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1414-[Omitted long context line]\nRULEBOOK.txt:1415:Example: “Basic Land — Mountain” means the card is a land with the subtype Mountain. “Creature — Goblin Wizard” means the card is a creature with the subtypes Goblin and Wizard. “Artifact — Equipment” means the card is an artifact with the subtype Equipment.\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1416-\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1417-205.3c If a card with multiple card types has one or more subtypes, each subtype is correlated to its appropriate card type.\n--\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1435-205.3k Instants and sorceries share their lists of subtypes; these subtypes are called spell types. The spell types are Adventure, Arcane, Lesson, Omen, and Trap.\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1436-\nRULEBOOK.txt:1437:[Omitted long matching line]\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1438-\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1439-[Omitted long context line]\n--\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1614-\nRULEBOOK.txt-1615-302.3. Creature subtypes are usually a single word long and are listed after a long dash: “Creature — Human Soldier,” “Artifact Creature — Golem,” and so on. Creature subtypes are also called creature types. Creatures may have multiple subtypes. See rule 205.3m for the complete list of creature types.\nRULEBOOK.txt:1616:Example: “Creature — Goblin Wizard” means the card is a creature with the subtypes Goblin and Wizard.\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1617-\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-1618-302.4. Power and toughness are characteristics only creatures have.\n--\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-6336-732.1b Occasionally the game gets into a state in which a set of actions could be repeated indefinitely (thus creating a “loop”). In that case, the shortcut rules can be used to determine how many times those actions are repeated without having to actually perform them, and how the loop is broken.\n/workspace/RULEBOOK.txt-6337-\n...\n</persisted-output>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BbDN9Vux561uQvk4PqzpZC","session_id":"bc7916e9-61b8-4183-92a0-8e53a58e330b","uuid":"f4e692a4-7492-4dc8-a491-464f380ea95d","timestamp":"2026-05-28T00:24:56.296Z","subagent_type":"Tester","task_description":"Tester for sos_57"}
