{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BPciWv6JLzpDmLhkmdprEP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n4\t    \"file\": \"engine/turn.py\",\n5\t    \"line\": 205,\n6\t    \"comment\": \"BeginningOfMainPhaseTriggeredEvent is only fired at (Phase.PRECOMBAT_MAIN, None) but never at (Phase.POSTCOMBAT_MAIN, None). The oracle text says 'at the beginning of your next main phase.' If ManaSculpt is cast during the controller's own precombat main phase (e.g., in response to a spell during pre-combat), the next main phase for that player would be postcombat main — but the event is never emitted there. More critically, any other scenario where postcombat main is the 'next' main phase for the controller will silently fail. The fix is to also fire BeginningOfMainPhaseTriggeredEvent at the start of POSTCOMBAT_MAIN, with the condition checking that it is the controller's main phase (which it always is for main phases in a 2-player game).\"\n7\t  },\n8\t  {\n9\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n10\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n11\t    \"line\": 144,\n12\t    \"comment\": \"The mana bonus is calculated using mana_cost.cmc (converted mana cost / mana value), but the oracle text says 'an amount of {C} equal to the amount of mana spent to cast that spell.' For spells with {X} in the cost, the CMC on the stack includes the chosen X value, but ManaCost.cmc treats X as 0 (per the cmc property definition: 'treating X as 0'). This means countering an {X}{R} spell cast for X=5 (6 mana spent) would grant only 1 {C} instead of 6. The fix should capture the actual mana spent, e.g. via a 'mana_spent' attribute on the StackObject if available, or at minimum document this as a known limitation. Even if the current test suite does not cover this X-cost edge case, it is a correctness gap relative to the spec's oracle text.\"\n13\t  },\n14\t  {\n15\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n16\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n17\t    \"line\": 43,\n18\t    \"comment\": \"_counter_spell accesses game.stack._items directly (private attribute, noted with noqa:SLF001). If the Stack class provides a public removal API, it should be used instead. If no public API exists this is acceptable, but the access is fragile against future engine refactors.\"\n19\t  },\n20\t  {\n21\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n22\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n23\t    \"line\": 106,\n24\t    \"comment\": \"can_cast uses 'card_types - {CardType.LAND}' to determine if something is a spell. This correctly excludes pure lands (which never go on the stack anyway), but a multi-type card like an Artifact Land would pass this filter. In practice this is harmless since lands never use the stack, but the comment and approach could be clearer — it would be more direct to check for the presence of INSTANT, SORCERY, CREATURE, ENCHANTMENT, ARTIFACT, or PLANESWALKER types rather than exclusion logic.\"\n25\t  },\n26\t  {\n27\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n28\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n29\t    \"line\": 165,\n30\t    \"comment\": \"The _condition closure captures 'controller' from the enclosing on_resolve scope. If the controller object is later reassigned or becomes invalid, the closure still holds a reference to the original object. This is the correct pattern given Python closure semantics, but it means there's no guard for the case where the card changes controller between resolution and the beginning of main phase (a rare edge case). This is advisory — the existing engine tests do not appear to exercise control-change mid-game.\"\n31\t  }\n32\t]\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc7916e9-61b8-4183-92a0-8e53a58e330b","uuid":"fe8d896b-42fe-4cb6-b10b-8a66d2799ed6","timestamp":"2026-05-28T00:32:33.005Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_57/review.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"engine/turn.py\",\n    \"line\": 205,\n    \"comment\": \"BeginningOfMainPhaseTriggeredEvent is only fired at (Phase.PRECOMBAT_MAIN, None) but never at (Phase.POSTCOMBAT_MAIN, None). The oracle text says 'at the beginning of your next main phase.' If ManaSculpt is cast during the controller's own precombat main phase (e.g., in response to a spell during pre-combat), the next main phase for that player would be postcombat main — but the event is never emitted there. More critically, any other scenario where postcombat main is the 'next' main phase for the controller will silently fail. The fix is to also fire BeginningOfMainPhaseTriggeredEvent at the start of POSTCOMBAT_MAIN, with the condition checking that it is the controller's main phase (which it always is for main phases in a 2-player game).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 144,\n    \"comment\": \"The mana bonus is calculated using mana_cost.cmc (converted mana cost / mana value), but the oracle text says 'an amount of {C} equal to the amount of mana spent to cast that spell.' For spells with {X} in the cost, the CMC on the stack includes the chosen X value, but ManaCost.cmc treats X as 0 (per the cmc property definition: 'treating X as 0'). This means countering an {X}{R} spell cast for X=5 (6 mana spent) would grant only 1 {C} instead of 6. The fix should capture the actual mana spent, e.g. via a 'mana_spent' attribute on the StackObject if available, or at minimum document this as a known limitation. Even if the current test suite does not cover this X-cost edge case, it is a correctness gap relative to the spec's oracle text.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 43,\n    \"comment\": \"_counter_spell accesses game.stack._items directly (private attribute, noted with noqa:SLF001). If the Stack class provides a public removal API, it should be used instead. If no public API exists this is acceptable, but the access is fragile against future engine refactors.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 106,\n    \"comment\": \"can_cast uses 'card_types - {CardType.LAND}' to determine if something is a spell. This correctly excludes pure lands (which never go on the stack anyway), but a multi-type card like an Artifact Land would pass this filter. In practice this is harmless since lands never use the stack, but the comment and approach could be clearer — it would be more direct to check for the presence of INSTANT, SORCERY, CREATURE, ENCHANTMENT, ARTIFACT, or PLANESWALKER types rather than exclusion logic.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_57/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 165,\n    \"comment\": \"The _condition closure captures 'controller' from the enclosing on_resolve scope. If the controller object is later reassigned or becomes invalid, the closure still holds a reference to the original object. This is the correct pattern given Python closure semantics, but it means there's no guard for the case where the card changes controller between resolution and the beginning of main phase (a rare edge case). This is advisory — the existing engine tests do not appear to exercise control-change mid-game.\"\n  }\n]\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}}}
