{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_017PPwp4yzPRe5o12zH7CADg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CHwgbhhXQ1Yzqd5t5xiZYd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_97/review.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_97/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 119,\n    \"comment\": \"The -2 ability's oracle text says 'from your graveyard' (the controller's graveyard), but the implementation iterates all players' graveyards to find the target card. After finding `source_player`, the code never validates that `source_player == controller`. A creature sitting in an opponent's graveyard would pass the containment check and be moved to the battlefield, violating the spec restriction. Fix: after finding `source_player`, add `if source_player is not controller: return` (or restrict the search to the controller's graveyard only).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_97/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 134,\n    \"comment\": \"The -7 ability captures `target_opponent` but never uses it after the None guard on line 134. The implementation skips opponent turns by appending the *controller's* seat index to `game.extra_turns` (lines 164-165). This gives extra turns to the controller rather than skipping the opponent's turns. In a 2-player game the net effect is accidentally equivalent only when both seat indices are considered, but the `target_opponent` variable — which determines *which* opponent to affect — is completely ignored. In a future multi-player context this would be wrong. Additionally, adding the controller's index to extra_turns inserts extra turns for the controller (who may already be taking a turn), not the opponent's seat. The correct approach is to either append the controller's seat index for each head (which does simulate 'skip opponent's next X turns' in 2-player) but then the target validation serves no purpose, or to properly implement skipping via opponent tracking.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_97/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 8,\n    \"comment\": \"`CardType` is imported from `engine.types` but is never referenced in the card body. The `Planeswalker` base class handles adding `CardType.PLANESWALKER` internally. This unused import is harmless but should be removed for cleanliness.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_97/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 56,\n    \"comment\": \"`get_loyalty_abilities()` defines all four ability closures and returns a new list every call. Because the closures capture `pw = self`, this is functionally safe, but calling `get_loyalty_abilities()` repeatedly in tests (as many tests do: `ral.get_loyalty_abilities()[N]`) creates new closures each time. This is not a bug but could be surprising. Consider caching the list in `__init__`.\"\n  }\n]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":583,"cache_read_input_tokens":29916,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":583,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RB2jmsiWCYz7MoemkNSCRJ","session_id":"bc7916e9-61b8-4183-92a0-8e53a58e330b","uuid":"8f6915fa-0921-4b54-bc26-2051f6d0e444","request_id":"req_011CbU3JzYo2QEpA25sD8RgG","subagent_type":"Reviewer","task_description":"Reviewer for sos_97"}
