{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W7zVBTWmpkiwBsCqHmZBSG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"card_id\": \"sos_226\",\n4\t    \"requirement\": \"copy the spell and you may choose new targets for the copy\",\n5\t    \"why_untestable\": \"Verifying that the copy can have fully-independent new targets chosen requires scripting DeterministicPlayer with choose_target calls in addition to choose_yes_no and choose_card, and asserting that the copy's chosen_targets differ from the original. The copy-spell machinery (engine.stack.copy_spell) exists, but wiring a full choose-new-targets flow in a deterministic test requires the implementer to expose which StackObject is the copy so the test can inspect its targets. Without a stable API handle on 'the copy just pushed', the assertion becomes fragile.\",\n6\t    \"what_would_unblock_it\": \"The implementation could return the new StackObject from the casualty effect, or store it as `self._last_copy` so tests can assert on copy.targets vs original.targets. Alternatively, the implementer could expose a 'copy_spell_and_choose_targets' helper that accepts a target-selector callable, making the interaction unit-testable.\"\n7\t  }\n8\t]\n9\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc7916e9-61b8-4183-92a0-8e53a58e330b","uuid":"2d5e3846-02a8-40d5-a64d-9a81883834c7","timestamp":"2026-05-28T01:14:31.400Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_226/untestable.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"card_id\": \"sos_226\",\n    \"requirement\": \"copy the spell and you may choose new targets for the copy\",\n    \"why_untestable\": \"Verifying that the copy can have fully-independent new targets chosen requires scripting DeterministicPlayer with choose_target calls in addition to choose_yes_no and choose_card, and asserting that the copy's chosen_targets differ from the original. The copy-spell machinery (engine.stack.copy_spell) exists, but wiring a full choose-new-targets flow in a deterministic test requires the implementer to expose which StackObject is the copy so the test can inspect its targets. Without a stable API handle on 'the copy just pushed', the assertion becomes fragile.\",\n    \"what_would_unblock_it\": \"The implementation could return the new StackObject from the casualty effect, or store it as `self._last_copy` so tests can assert on copy.targets vs original.targets. Alternatively, the implementer could expose a 'copy_spell_and_choose_targets' helper that accepts a target-selector callable, making the interaction unit-testable.\"\n  }\n]\n","numLines":9,"startLine":1,"totalLines":9}}}
