st assertions about trigger registration. While the implementation is functionally correct, a comment or docstring explaining this architectural split (static ability implemented via active_cost_reducers, trigger only for observability/test compliance) would reduce confusion for future maintainers.\"\n19\t  },\n20\t  {\n21\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n22\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_245/card_impl.py\",\n23\t    \"line\": 164,\n24\t    \"comment\": \"The active_cost_reducers entry is never removed when Witherbloom leaves the battlefield. This is acknowledged in KEY_DECISIONS.md as a known limitation. The _reducer_reduction function mitigates correctness issues by checking bf.contains(source), but the stale entry still iterates on every spell cast, which is a minor performance concern. A LeavesBattlefieldTriggeredEvent handler (like the one added in sos_201) could clean this up and would be consistent with the lifecycle management convention.\"\n25\t  }\n26\t]\n27\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc7916e9-61b8-4183-92a0-8e53a58e330b","uuid":"9ff1f1ac-3241-4d06-8348-24ad0d396a16","timestamp":"2026-05-28T01:33:34.810Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_245/review.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"engine/casting.py\",\n    \"line\": 120,\n    \"comment\": \"The broad `except Exception: pass` block in the active_cost_reducers loop silently swallows all exceptions from condition and reduction callables. This means bugs such as AttributeError or TypeError inside a reducer will be silently ignored, making them invisible in tests and production. At minimum the exception should be logged; a narrow except (e.g. catching only specific expected errors) would be more appropriate here.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_245/card_impl.py\",\n    \"line\": 164,\n    \"comment\": \"register_triggers() appends a new entry to game.active_cost_redu